@page "/"

<PageTitle>Backlog UI Storybook</PageTitle>

<StoryPage Title="Backlog UI Storybook"
           Summary="Every component in Backlog.UI.Components, rendered on its own.">

    <Story Name="What this is"
           Description="Why the library exists and what this host is for.">
        <div class="sb-stack">
            <p>
                <code>Backlog.UI.Components</code> is a Razor Class Library with no domain in it.
                It knows about buttons, inputs, markdown blocks and tree rows — it does not know
                about entries, backlogs or repositories. Anything that would need that vocabulary
                stays in the app.
            </p>
            <p>
                This storybook references the library and <code>Backlog.Aspire.ServiceDefaults</code>
                and nothing else. If a component ever grew a dependency on a module, this project
                would stop compiling — which is the cheapest possible test of that boundary.
            </p>
            <p>
                It is also the surface to review the components on: they render here exactly as
                they render in the app, because the storybook links the library stylesheet and
                adds only page chrome of its own.
            </p>
            <p>
                What is <em>not</em> here is the design and UX rules. Those live in the repository's
                <code>.design</code> folder, and every page in this storybook links the chapters
                that govern it from a <strong>Design rules</strong> block in its header — so a page
                says how a component is used, and one click away says what it is allowed to look
                like. A rule restated here would be a second copy of a rule nobody diffs against
                the first, which is how the two used to disagree.
            </p>
            <p>
                <code>color-scheme.md</code> and <code>typography-and-layout.md</code> hold the
                tokens <a href="foundations">Foundations</a> measures,
                <code>interaction-guidelines.md</code> holds the save, reorder, density and
                timeline behaviour, <code>content-editing.md</code> holds what may be done to a
                markdown body, and <code>accessibility.md</code> holds the bar every component
                clears. Each of those files names the pages here that show it, and says which of
                its rules are not built yet.
            </p>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="How to run it"
           Description="Standalone, or as an Aspire resource next to the other harnesses."
           Code="@RunCode">
        <div class="sb-stack">
            <p>
                Under the AppHost it is the <code>ui-storybook</code> resource. It starts
                automatically and waits for nothing, because it depends on nothing.
            </p>
            <p>
                Standalone, Kestrel picks the port and prints it. Mermaid is the one thing
                fetched from a CDN, on first use, so on a machine with no egress the
                <a href="diagrams">Diagrams</a> and
                <a href="markdown/diagrams">Diagrams in markdown</a> pages fall back to
                showing their source instead of a picture — unless the host has pointed
                <code>window.backlogDiagramLibrarySources</code> at a local copy. The
                <a href="graph-explorer">graph explorer</a> and
                <a href="graph-atlas">graph atlas</a> draw on their own canvas, so they
                render offline, and so does everything else.
            </p>
        </div>
    </Story>

    <Story Name="Component groups"
           Description="In the order they can be read: the parts first — input and action, structure, feedback, content, knowledge metadata — then the subjects each of which is a convention rather than a component (Integrations, Task list, Roadmap, Dashboard), then Combined usage, where whole surfaces are composed out of everything above. Nothing is shown before its parts, so a page only ever uses components a page above it has already introduced.">
        @* The loop variable is not called "page": Razor reads @page.Href as the
           @page directive and the file stops compiling. *@
        <div class="sb-grid">
            @foreach (var link in StorybookIndex.ComponentPages)
            {
                <a class="sb-card-link" href="@link.Href">
                    <span class="sb-card-link__title">@link.Title</span>
                    <span class="sb-card-link__text">@link.Summary</span>
                </a>
            }
        </div>
    </Story>

</StoryPage>

@code {
    private const string RunCode = """
        # standalone
        dotnet run --project src/Harness/Backlog.UI.Storybook

        # with everything else
        aspire run
        """;
}
